---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ContentSection from '../components/ContentSection.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import LinkCard from '../components/LinkCard.astro';

const relatedPages = [
  { title: 'Responsible Betting', href: '/responsible-betting', description: 'Protecting your bankroll and well-being' },
  { title: 'Betting Strategy', href: '/betting-strategy', description: 'Framework for disciplined betting' },
  { title: 'How Odds Work', href: '/how-betting-odds-work', description: 'Understanding odds and value' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Common Sports Betting Mistakes"
  description="The most common behavioral, emotional, and structural mistakes sports bettors make and how to avoid them."
>
  <Hero
    title="Common Sports Betting Mistakes"
    subtitle="Behavioral, Emotional, and Structural Errors That Cost Bettors Money"
    size="medium"
  />

  <div class="container content-container">
    <article class="page-content">
      <ContentSection>
        <div class="quick-take">
          <h2>Quick Take</h2>
          <p>
            Most sports bettors lose not because they can't analyze games but because they make avoidable process mistakes. Chasing losses, ignoring bankroll management, betting too many games, and letting emotions drive decisions are far more damaging than occasionally being wrong about a game. This guide catalogs the most common errors across behavioral, emotional, and structural categories—recognizing them is the first step to avoiding them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Why Do Most Bettors Lose?" id="why-lose">
        <p>
          The vig guarantees that bettors as a whole lose money. But the distribution of outcomes is uneven—some bettors lose much more than the vig would predict. These excess losses come from process errors, not bad luck or lack of sports knowledge.
        </p>
        <p>
          The mistakes below are consistent across all types of bettors. Even experienced, knowledgeable sports fans make them. Recognition is protection.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What Are the Behavioral Mistakes?" id="behavioral">
        <h3>Betting too many games</h3>
        <p>
          Every bet exposes you to the vig. Unless you have a clear edge on every game, volume hurts your returns. Many bettors feel they need action on every slate, but selectivity is a virtue. The best bettors pass on far more games than they bet.
        </p>

        <h3>Not line shopping</h3>
        <p>
          Betting at a single sportsbook means accepting whatever price they offer. Getting -110 instead of -105 seems small, but over hundreds of bets, it's the difference between marginal losses and potential profits. Every half-point matters.
        </p>

        <h3>Ignoring bankroll management</h3>
        <p>
          Betting 10% or 20% of your bankroll on single games—especially after wins—is a path to ruin. Variance is real. Even good bets lose. Flat betting 1-3% units protects you from inevitable losing streaks.
        </p>

        <h3>Chasing losses</h3>
        <p>
          After losing, the urge to bet larger amounts to "get even" is powerful and destructive. Chasing accelerates losses and compounds bad decisions. If you're down, the disciplined response is to maintain unit size, not increase it.
        </p>

        <h3>Parlaying everything</h3>
        <p>
          Parlays are high-margin bets for sportsbooks. While the potential payouts are attractive, the combined probability of hitting multiple legs makes them poor expected value in most cases. Occasional parlays for entertainment are fine; relying on them is costly.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What Are the Emotional Mistakes?" id="emotional">
        <h3>Betting on your favorite team</h3>
        <p>
          Fan bias is real. Betting on teams you root for introduces emotional attachment that compromises objectivity. You might still be able to analyze the game fairly, but most bettors overestimate their ability to separate fandom from analysis.
        </p>

        <h3>Revenge betting</h3>
        <p>
          After a bad beat or frustrating loss, the impulse to "get back at" the sportsbook or the sport by betting more is irrational. The sportsbook doesn't know or care about your last bet. Each wager is independent.
        </p>

        <h3>Overconfidence after wins</h3>
        <p>
          Winning streaks feel like validation of skill, even when variance is the main driver. Overconfidence leads to larger bets, less careful analysis, and eventually, larger losses. Humility protects your bankroll.
        </p>

        <h3>Betting while emotional or impaired</h3>
        <p>
          Anger, frustration, excitement, alcohol, and fatigue all impair judgment. If you're not in a calm, analytical state, you shouldn't be placing bets. The sportsbook will still be there tomorrow.
        </p>

        <h3>Results-oriented thinking</h3>
        <p>
          Judging bets by whether they won rather than whether they were good decisions leads to false conclusions. Bad bets can win; good bets can lose. Process matters more than any single outcome.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <Callout type="info" title="The Best Process Feels Boring">
        <p>
          Disciplined betting—flat units, selective plays, careful analysis—isn't exciting. If your betting feels thrilling and action-packed, you're probably making emotional decisions. The goal is good decisions, not entertainment.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <ContentSection title="What Are the Structural Mistakes?" id="structural">
        <h3>Not understanding the vig</h3>
        <p>
          Many bettors don't realize that -110 odds require 52.4% accuracy just to break even. The vig is your cost of participation. Ignoring it means ignoring the fundamental challenge of betting.
        </p>

        <h3>Misunderstanding expected value</h3>
        <p>
          A bet isn't "good" just because you think it will win. It's good if your estimated probability exceeds the implied probability in the odds. Without this framework, you're gambling on gut feel, not making informed decisions.
        </p>

        <h3>Relying on public "sharp" data</h3>
        <p>
          Websites claiming to show sharp vs public splits are providing estimates at best. Using this imprecise data as your primary decision-making tool is building on a weak foundation.
        </p>

        <h3>Following touts and picks services</h3>
        <p>
          Most picks sellers use manipulated records, cherry-picked results, or simply guess. Even legitimate services face the problem that by the time picks are distributed, lines have moved and value is gone. Outsourcing your thinking rarely works.
        </p>

        <h3>Ignoring closing line value</h3>
        <p>
          Tracking whether your bets beat the closing line is one of the best ways to measure skill. If you consistently bet worse numbers than the market closes at, your process needs work—even if you're winning short-term.
        </p>

        <h3>Focusing on win rate over ROI</h3>
        <p>
          A bettor winning 60% at -200 odds is losing money. A bettor winning 45% at +150 odds is profitable. Win rate without context is meaningless. Return on investment (ROI) and units won are better measures.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What This Means for Bettors" id="what-means">
        <p>
          Avoiding these mistakes won't guarantee profits, but it removes unnecessary obstacles:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Be selective:</strong> Only bet when you believe you have an edge. Passing is a valid decision.</li>
          <li><strong>Manage your bankroll:</strong> Flat bet small units. Survive variance.</li>
          <li><strong>Line shop:</strong> Get the best available price on every bet.</li>
          <li><strong>Stay emotionally neutral:</strong> Don't bet angry, drunk, or on tilt.</li>
          <li><strong>Track your bets:</strong> Honest record-keeping reveals patterns and mistakes.</li>
          <li><strong>Focus on process:</strong> Judge decisions by quality, not outcomes.</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Common Misconceptions" id="misconceptions">
        <h3>"I've been watching sports my whole life—I know enough"</h3>
        <p>
          Sports knowledge and betting skill are different. Many knowledgeable fans are terrible bettors because they don't understand odds, value, or bankroll management.
        </p>

        <h3>"If I bet more games, I'll find more winners"</h3>
        <p>
          Volume amplifies the vig, not your edge. Unless you have a systematic edge across many games, fewer selective bets will outperform high-volume spray.
        </p>

        <h3>"I can feel when a game is a lock"</h3>
        <p>
          Confidence is not evidence. Every "lock" can lose. Overconfident bets tend to be oversized bets, compounding the damage when they lose.
        </p>

        <h3>"My system just needs a few more wins to prove itself"</h3>
        <p>
          Confirmation bias keeps bettors attached to losing systems. Results over a small sample don't prove or disprove anything. Track performance over hundreds of bets before drawing conclusions.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="When This Matters Most" id="when-matters">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>After losses:</strong> The urge to chase and deviate from process is strongest when down.</li>
          <li><strong>During big events:</strong> Super Bowl, March Madness, and playoffs create pressure to bet more and bigger.</li>
          <li><strong>On your favorite teams:</strong> Emotional attachment clouds judgment on games you care about most.</li>
          <li><strong>When "action" feels necessary:</strong> The feeling that you "need" to bet on a slate is a warning sign.</li>
          <li><strong>After winning streaks:</strong> Overconfidence peaks when things are going well.</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Key Takeaways" id="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Most losses come from process errors, not bad analysis</li>
          <li>Behavioral mistakes: betting too many games, ignoring line shopping, poor bankroll management, chasing losses</li>
          <li>Emotional mistakes: fan bias, revenge betting, overconfidence, betting while impaired</li>
          <li>Structural mistakes: ignoring vig, misunderstanding EV, following touts, focusing on win rate over ROI</li>
          <li>Disciplined betting feels boring—if it's exciting, something is probably wrong</li>
          <li>Recognizing these mistakes is the first step to avoiding them</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Related Resources" id="related">
        <div class="related-grid">
          {relatedPages.map((page) => (
            <LinkCard
              title={page.title}
              href={page.href}
              description={page.description}
            />
          ))}
        </div>
      </ContentSection>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-content {
    padding: var(--spacing-3xl) 0;
  }

  .quick-take {
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding: var(--spacing-lg);
    border-radius: 0 var(--border-radius) var(--border-radius) 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  .quick-take h2 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
  }

  .quick-take p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .related-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: var(--spacing-md);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .related-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
